\n\nFor club grouping: key = base|state. When state is None (e.g., FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" → alias gives state MG; FIFA \"Botafogo\" → \"botafogo|None\"; Brasileirao \"Botafogo-RJ\" → \"botafogo|RJ\"). Are \"botafogo|None\" and \"botafogo|RJ\" the same club? Yes (FIFA Botafogo = Botafogo-RJ, the Rio club, since Botafogo-PB isn't in FIFA 19... probably). But programmatically, None-state variants with a base that has exactly ONE state-qualified club should merge into it. If ambiguous (america|None), keep separate or merge into the most frequent? Hmm. \"América\" bare in some dataset — which club? Context-dependent. \n\nPragmatic approach for the demo: \n- `ClubRegistry` holds clubs keyed by canonical key (base|state).\n- When registering a variant with no state: if exactly one existing club shares the base → merge. If multiple exist → merge into the one with the most match records? That could mis-attribute. OR keep it unmerged as its own club with display name as-is. For query resolution: resolve(\"America\") with no state → return all clubs matching base \"america\" (MG and RN), user can refine; best-match = most records.\n\nFor match queries \"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\" — resolve(\"Palmeiras\") → palmeiras|SP (and palmeiras|None merges into it). Works.\n\nFor head-to-head \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\" — both resolve uniquely.\n\nFor stats \"Corinthians home record 2022\" — resolve → corinthians|SP. novo has bare \"Corinthians\" (no state) — merges since only one corinthians club. \n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with merging: I decide merges at load time. Order matters. Let me do: load ALL match rows first, collecting variants. Then:\n1. Group variants by base.\n2. For each base: state-qualified variants → distinct clubs.\n3. No-state variants → if exactly one state-qualified club with that base exists → merge into it; if none → own club; if multiple → own club (ambiguous), flagged.\n4. Apply alias map BEFORE all this.\n\nAlias map (spaceless base → target base + state):\n- \"atleticomineiro\" → (\"atletico\",\"MG\")\n- \"atleticomineiro\" (accent-folded same) — accent folding makes them identical anyway.\n- \"atleticoparanaense\"/\"athleticoparanaense\" → (\"atletico\",\"PR\"); also \"athleticopr\" → (\"atletico\",\"PR\")? \"Athletico-PR\" → base \"athletico\" state PR. If I don't alias, \"athletico|PR\" and \"atletico|PR\" are two clubs. Must alias base \"athletico\" → \"atletico\". But is there any OTHER athletico? No. So base alias: \"athletico\" → \"atletico\". \n- \"sportclubdorecife\" → (\"sport\",\"PE\"); \"sportrecife\" → (\"sport\",\"PE\")\n- \"americafc\" — from \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" after dropping parens → alias → (\"america\",\"MG\"). And \"americafcnatal\" → (\"america\",\"RN\"). Also BR-Football \"America FC Natal\"? I saw \"America FC Natal\" in the list — yes. → (\"america\",\"RN\").\n- \"cearascarb\" no — FIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"cearasc\" → (\"ceara\",\"CE\"). Base \"ceara\" with state CE from Brasileirao \"Ceara-CE\" — alias to match.\n- \"saopaulofc\"? Is there such? FIFA club name for São Paulo is \"São Paulo\" I think. Check later.\n- \"goias\"? fine as-is.\n- \"parana\" (FIFA \"Paraná\") → (\"parana\",\"PR\")? Brasileirao has \"Parana-SC\"?? No wait — Paraná Clube is PR state. Let me check \"Parana-SC\"... I saw 'Avai-SC' (SC = Santa Catarina, correct for Avaí). Paraná would be \"Parana-PR\". Need to check data.\n- \"vitoria\" — FIFA \"Vitória\" (BA). Brasileirao \"Vitoria-BA\". Base \"vitoria\" state BA. Unique? Vitória-ES exists in lower divisions (Cup data \"Vitoria - ES\"?). If both exist, FIFA bare \"vitoria\" is ambiguous-flagged. FIFA's Vitória is the BA one. Alias... I can't distinguish. Leave as ambiguous merge → stays separate club \"vitoria|None\" with fewer records. Hmm, that means \"Which players play for Vitória?\" resolves to FIFA's club fine (players attach via club key), but head-to-head would include the None club only if merged... \n\nOK wait, I'm overengineering the merge logic. Let me reconsider.\n\n**Two separate concerns:**\n1. **Match data team matching** — user query → set of team variants in match datasets.\n2. **Player data club matching** — user query → FIFA club string.\n\nFor (1), the cleanest: a resolver that maps a user-supplied name to a *set of variant keys* (base|state, plus base|None entries). A match row belongs to a team if the row's variant key is in the resolved set OR the row's base is in the resolved bases... Hmm.\n\nActually, let me flip it: **for match queries, match on BASE only (ignoring state), unless the query specifies a state.** So resolve(\"Flamengo\") → base \"flamengo\" → all match rows with base \"flamengo\" (flamengo|RJ, flamengo|None). resolve(\"America-MG\") → base \"america\" + state MG → only rows with base \"america\" AND state MG. This is simple and robust! No merging needed for match queries at all. The only cost: bare ambiguous queries (\"America\") span both clubs. For stats/standings I need club identity though (can't have América-MG and América-RN mixed in a standings table).\n\nHmm, but standings: for a given competition+season, each team plays under ONE variant anyway. Standings computed per (competition, season) — group rows by variant key. Different variants of same club within one season+competition shouldn't happen (novo \"Athletico-PR\" 2019 vs Brasileirao_Matches \"Atletico-PR\" 2019 — but those are DIFFERENT datasets covering the same competition! novo covers 2003-2019, Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2021. Both have Brasileirão 2012-2019 — DUPLICATE data!).\n\nOh wait, that's important. novo_campeonato_brasileiro (2003-2019) and Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2021) overlap 2012-2019! If I query \"all Flamengo matches\" across all files, I'd double-count. And BR-Football-Dataset also has \"Serie A\" 2014-2023 — triple coverage!\n\nHow to handle? The spec says \"Search all match files for Palmeiras matches\" for \"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\" — implying cross-file search is desired. For head-to-head \"Flamengo 12 wins, ...\" — from the example, they seem to just count whatever's in the dataset. For standings \"2019 Brasileirão\" — computed from matches; if I include both novo and Brasileirao_Matches 2019, double-counted standings = wrong.\n\nDesign decision: **each dataset is a distinct source; queries default to a primary source per competition, with optional source filter; head-to-head and stats deduplicate identical matches (same date+teams+score).**\n\nDedup approach: match identity = (date, home_base, away_base, home_goals, away_goals). novo \"29/03/2003\" vs Brasileirao \"2003-...\" — novo covers 2003-2019, Brasileirao covers 2012-2021: for a 2015 Flamengo vs Vasco match on the same date with the same score in both files → duplicate. Dedup on (date, home, away) — a real fixture can't happen twice on the same date. But state-suffix variants: \"Vasco\" (novo, base vasco|None) vs \"Vasco-RJ\" (Brasileirao, vasco|RJ) — normalize to base only for identity: (date, \"vasco\", \"flamengo\"). \n\nSo: **canonical match identity uses base names (no state), date, and goals.** For \"find all matches\" queries, dedup by identity, preferring the richer record. For standings per competition+season, dedup likewise. Round-robin leagues: 38 rounds × 2 legs, each fixture (home,away) appears once per leg — dedup by (season, home, away, date)... two legs have different dates and swapped home/away, so (date, home, away) is safe.\n\nBut wait — BR-Football \"Serie A\" 2014-2023 vs Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2021: same fixtures, dates might differ by a day (postponed/re-scheduled) or kickoff times differ. If dates differ, dedup fails and we double-count. Risk accepted — for standings I'll dedup by (season, competition-normalized, home_base, away_base) keeping first occurrence? Hmm, but Copa do Brasil has home/away legs — same fixture twice! For cup competitions (Copa do Brasil, Libertadores knockout), (home, away) legitimately repeats across legs on different dates. So dedup key must include date.\n\nDecision: \n- **Dedup key = (date, home_base, away_base)**. Same fixture on same date = same match even if goals parse differently (they won't). \n- Standings: computed from a chosen source per (competition, season) — prefer the dedicated dataset (Brasileirao_Matches for Brasileirão; novo for 2003-2011 since Brasileirao_Matches starts 2012; BR-Football Serie A as fallback/for 2022-2023). Hmm — but simpler: compute standings over the deduped union of all sources for that competition+season. If dates align between sources, dedup handles it. Let me actually check overlap: compare novo 2019 rows vs Brasileirao_Matches 2019 rows for date alignment.\n\nActually, let me simplify the whole architecture: **A single unified Match index with dedup.** Load all 5 match files into MatchRecord objects with fields: date, home_team (display), away_team (display), home_base, away_base, home_state, away_state, home_goals, away_goals, competition, season, round, stage, source, stadium, plus extended stats (corners, shots, attacks, halftime) when available. Dedup across sources by (date, home_base, away_base) keeping the record with more info / from the preferred source. But BR-Football has extra stats (corners etc.) — if deduped away, we lose them. Preference order for keeping: record with extended stats > dedicated dataset > novo? But then \"source\" attribution gets fuzzy for display (\"Brasileirão Round 22\" example). \n\nHmm wait, actually — do the dates align between BR-Football and Brasileirao_Matches? BR-Football dates are plain \"2023-09-24\" (no time). Brasileirao \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\". Date-only comparison needed: normalize date to ISO date (datetime.date). Different sources might have a match on 2023-09-24 vs 2023-09-25 for the same fixture... Let me check with data.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is cross-source dedup even needed for the core queries? \n\n- \"Show me all Flamengo vs Fluminense matches\" — example shows \"(15 more matches in dataset)\" with head-to-head counts. If we double-count, numbers are wrong-ish. The spec example says \"Head-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins...\" — \"in dataset\" language suggests they don't care much. But correctness matters for standings (\"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" — Flamengo, 90 pts. If double-counted, points would be ~180). So dedup IS needed for standings at least.\n\nPlan: unified index with dedup by (date, home_base, away_base); keep- preference: (1) has extended stats, (2) source priority [Brasileirao_Matches > novo for Brasileirão; dedicated cup/libertadores files; BR-Football last]. Wait but BR-Football Serie A covers 2022-2023 which Brasileirao_Matches (ends 2021... let me verify: seasons list said 2012-2021. Hmm earlier output: seasons: ['2012'...'2021']. And the spec example mentions \"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\" — answerable via BR-Football Serie A (2023 present, date range to 2023-12-07). Good — so BR-Football matters for 2022-2023 Brasileirão.\n\nStandings for Brasileirão 2019: sources novo(2019) + Brasileirao_Matches(2019) + BR-Football Serie A(2019). Dedup by date+teams: do novo and Brasileirao_Matches agree on dates? Both from same Kaggle author (ricardomattos05) — novo is from a different author (macedojleo). Dates could differ (DD/MM/YYYY vs ISO). If they disagree by a day, double-count. Let me check the actual data overlap right now before finalizing. Let me write a quick script.\n\nAlso check: does BR-Football Serie A have every Brasileirao_Matches fixture? BR-Football: 3689 Serie A matches over 2014-2023. A full season = 380 matches. 2014-2021 = 8 seasons × 380 = 3040, plus 2022-2023 = 760 → 3800 > 3689. So BR-Football is incomplete (missing some matches). Brasileirao_Matches: 4180 = 11 seasons × 380 = 4180. Complete! novo: 6886 vs 17 seasons × 380 = 6460... 6886 > 6460? 2003-2019 = 17 seasons. Hmm 2003-2005 had more teams (2003: 26 teams?, 2004: 24, 2005: 22). Whatever.\n\nSo the primary source for Brasileirão standings 2012-2021 = Brasileirao_Matches; 2003-2011 = novo; 2022-2023 = BR-Football. But rather than hardcoding source selection, dedup should handle it IF dates align. Let me check date alignment between sources for a sample.\n\nLet me run a quick analysis script.\n\nThen the **MCP server tools**:\n\n1. `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)` → list of matches + count.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season)` → match list + W/D/L summary.\n3. `team_stats(team, competition, season, venue)` → matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate.\n4. `standings(competition, season)` → full table computed from matches, with points, W/D/L, GF/GA, GD, champion/relegation flags. Include per-competition logic: Brasileirão = round-robin (3 pts win); Copa do Brasil/Libertadores = knockout (standings don't make sense — but group stage for Libertadores could have mini-tables... too complex; for cups, return \"not a league\" or the final match / winner). Actually \"Who won the 2018 Copa Libertadores?\" → find the 'final' stage match(es) → winner. And \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" → Cup data has rounds 1-8; round 8 = final? Rounds are 1-8... The final would be the highest round in a season. Let me check: for each season, max round. If round 8 = final (2 legs). I'll infer: for a season, the final = matches in the max round of that season.\n5. `find_player(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit)` → player records.\n6. `top_players(nationality, club, position, limit, by)` → sorted by overall.\n7. `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)` → sorted by goal margin.\n8. `average_goals(competition, season)` → avg goals/match, home win rate etc.\n9. `competition_info(competition)` → seasons available, matches count, sources, champion per season maybe.\n10. `list_teams(competition, season)` → teams in that competition/season.\n11. `last_match(team, opponent)` → most recent match between teams (\"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\").\n12. Maybe `derbies(season)` — \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" — derbies = matches between traditional rivals. I can hardcode a list of famous Brazilian derbies (Fla-Flu, Palmeiras x Corinthians (Paulista), Gre-Nal, Clássico dos Milhões Vasco x Flamengo, Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro (Mineiro), Ba-Vi (Bahia x Vitória), etc.) and find matches between those pairs. That's a nice touch for the \"derbies\" example question.\n\nAlso a `resolve_team` helper tool maybe — or fold into responses (\"Did you mean América-MG or América-RN?\").\n\n**Knowledge graph framing**: The overview says \"provides a knowledge graph interface\". MCP tools returning structured JSON is the practical interpretation; I'll include entities (teams, players, matches) with relations in a lightweight graph layer — e.g., the TeamRegistry/Club entities with aliases = graph nodes; matches = edges between team nodes; players linked to clubs. I'll structure the code so there's a `graph.py` or just document the model. Keep it pragmatic: dataclasses + registry. I could add a `soccer_graph` tool returning nodes/edges for a query... I'll include `head_to_head` and stats as graph-derived. I think tool-level JSON with clear structure satisfies this. Maybe I'll add a simple `get_team_profile` tool that aggregates everything known about a club across all sources (matches, stats, players at that club) — that's the \"knowledge graph node\" view and covers cross-file queries (\"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\").\n\n**MCP server implementation**: Use `mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP`. Expose tools with typed signatures. Also make the server importable and testable without running STDIO. Entry point: `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp` or `server.py` script at root. I'll write `server.py` at root that imports the package and runs. Also `pyproject.toml` with a console script entry.\n\nWait — \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" I'll keep everything in the current dir: `server.py` (entry), package dir `brazilian_soccer_mcp/`, `tests/`. That satisfies \"current directory\" as the project root.\n\n**Testing**: BDD GWT-structured pytest — Given/When/Then in test names or comments, one test per scenario. Spec shows Gherkin. I'll write tests structured with `given_/when/then` helpers or docstrings: e.g.\n\n```python\nclass TestMatchQueries:\n    def test_given_match_data_loaded_when_search_flamengo_vs_fluminense_then_matches_with_date_scores_competition(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd a Gherkin feature file for documentation. Use fixtures that load the real data (module-scoped for performance). Tests must pass with real CSVs. Also test the MCP server tools directly by calling the underlying functions (not through stdio). Maybe one test that constructs the FastMCP server and lists tools.\n\nPerformance criteria: simple lookups < 2s, aggregates < 5s. In-memory with module-level cached index: loading 30k rows takes < 2s; queries are scans over 20k matches = milliseconds. Use `functools.lru_cache` / module singleton for the dataset. \n\n**Date parsing**: handle \"2023-09-24\", \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"29/03/2003\", \"29/03/2003 16:00\" etc. Write `parse_date` trying formats.\n\n**Numbers**: goals may be strings (\"2\"), ints, or floats (\"1.0\" in BR-Football home_goal \"1.0\"). Handle: int(float(x)).\n\nMissing values: BR-Football may have empty goals for future matches? Dates up to 2023-12-07, all past. Some rows may have empty date? Handle gracefully.\n\nLet me now check data alignment and some details before writing code.\n\nChecks to run:\n1. novo vs Brasileirao_Matches date alignment for a shared season (e.g., 2015 Flamengo matches).\n2. BR-Football vs Brasileirao_Matches alignment (2019).\n3. Cup max round per season = final? (e.g., 2017 season max round).\n4. Libertadores 'final' stage rows.\n5. FIFA clubs list (all clubs containing Brazilian teams; is \"Flamengo\" there?).\n6. BR-Football ht_result/at_result values (WIN/DRAW/LOST? saw DRAW, LOST, WON).\n7. Any empty goals values across files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d29ff0a001u0pVEADW2cYid3","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_05d26db4f001a1u6fW9WDNJrdS"}}
