/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n# Libertadores finals\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nfinals = [r for r in rows if r['stage'] == 'final']\nprint(\"Libertadores finals:\", len(finals))\nfor r in finals[:8]:\n    print(r['season'], r['datetime'][:10], r['home_team'], r['home_goal'], '-', r['away_goal'], r['away_team'])\n# NA season\nna = [r for r in rows if r['season'] == 'NA']\nprint(\"NA season rows:\", len(na), na[:2])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788269514424,"end":1788269514427}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So in 2015:\n- novo has \"Vasco\" and bm has \"Vasco da Gama\" (alias needed) and novo has \"Athletico\" vs bm \"atletico\" (alias needed).\n- With aliases, ~380/380 would match. 342 loosely matched even without aliases. Only 80 common dates though — meaning many matches have DIFFERENT dates between the two sources! Let me check: 380 matches each, loose matched 342 (with containment). Hmm wait, \"loose matched: 342\" counts bm keys that found a same-date loose match in novo. So 38 didn't match by date. But both have 380. So dates differ for ~38 matches (data entry differences between sources).\n\nImplication: dedup by (date, teams) will miss ~10% of overlaps between novo and bm. That's bad for standings if I merge sources.\n\nDecision: **don't merge sources silently for league standings.** Instead:\n- Standings for a (competition, season): pick the BEST single source for that season (most matches for that competition+season), compute from it. \n- \"Find matches\" queries: search across all sources but dedup by (date, home, away) — imperfect dedup is fine for listing (worst case a duplicate fixture listed twice with slightly different dates, acceptable; actually no — for head-to-head W/D/L counts, dupes skew).\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider: For head-to-head, which source(s) to use? If I use \"all sources\", Flamengo x Fluminense would count 2012-2019 twice (novo + bm) + 2014-2019 again (BR-Football Serie A). The example says \"Head-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\" — for Fla-Flu that's 27 matches, roughly 1.5 seasons of meetings (2 per season) — suggesting maybe just Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2021 would give ~20 meetings). Unclear. But correctness-wise, double counting is wrong.\n\n**Final architecture decision on sources**: Build the unified match index with a **source-priority dedup**:\n1. First, dedup EXACT within-source (no-op, they're clean).\n2. Cross-source: for Brasileirão Serie A: for seasons covered by multiple sources, prefer ONE source per (competition, season): the one with most matches (Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2021: 380/season complete; novo for 2003-2011; BR-Football for 2022-2023 where bm has nothing).\n   - bm 2021? seasons list showed 2012-2021 — but is 2021 complete? 4180 total = 11 × 380 exactly. Complete.\n   - BR-Football Serie A 2022: let me count. If BR-Football has 2022-2023 Serie A, good.\n3. So the \"canonical Brasileirão\" timeline: novo 2003-2011, bm 2012-2021, BR-Football 2022-2023.\n4. For Copa do Brasil: dedicated Brazilian_Cup_Matches (2012-2021) + BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\" (2014-2023, 1123 matches vs 1337 dedicated). Prefer dedicated for 2012-2021, BR-Football for 2022-2023.\n5. Libertadores: dedicated file only (BR-Football doesn't have Libertadores — tournaments: Copa do Brasil, Serie A, Serie B, Serie C only).\n6. Serie B and Serie C: only in BR-Football.\n\nBut ALSO keep the raw per-source records for \"extended stats\" queries (corners, shots, halftime) — those come from BR-Football regardless.\n\nHmm wait, but this \"prefer dedicated source for 2012-2021 Copa do Brasil\" — what if BR-Football has a match the dedicated file lacks? Edge case, ignore. Actually — better dedup rule that handles everything uniformly:\n\n**For each (competition, season): choose the authoritative source = source with the most matches for that (competition, season). Only include matches from that source in the canonical index for that competition-season.** With a tie, prefer dedicated > BR-Football > novo? For 2012-2019 Serie A: bm has 380, novo has 380 → tie → prefer bm (dedicated, has round info in consistent format). Actually prefer order: Brasileirao_Matches > Brazilian_Cup_Matches > Libertadores_Matches (dedicated files) > BR-Football > novo. Wait but for 2003-2011 Serie A, only novo has it → novo wins. For 2012-2019, bm 380 = novo 380 → bm wins. \n\nHmm, but hold on — what about \"Serie A\" naming? Competition normalization: \"Brasileirão Serie A\" from bm/novo; BR-Football \"Serie A\". Same competition. Map both to \"Brasileirão Serie A\". BR-Football \"Serie B\" → \"Brasileirão Serie B\". \"Copa do Brasil\" from both → \"Copa do Brasil\". Libertadores → \"Copa Libertadores\".\n\nThen: `matches` canonical index = for each (competition, season), take the authoritative source's matches. Keep extended stats attached by joining BR-Football rows (match by date+teams loose?) — complexity not worth it. Simpler: extended stats accessible via a separate query on BR-Football only (match_statistics tool), and the canonical index keeps whichever record won.\n\nHmm, actually — wait. Let me reconsider using BR-Football Serie A as authoritative when bm also covers (2014-2021): bm has round numbers, BR-Football doesn't but has stats. bm preferred. For 2022-2023 Serie A: BR-Football only. OK.\n\nAnd for match listing queries with source filter, users can query `source=BR-Football` to see stats-included records.\n\nLet me now also decide the ** standings computation**:\n- Round-robin competitions (Serie A, Serie B, Serie C): 3-1-0 points, rank by pts, W, GD, GF. Mark top-1 as champion (Serie A); bottom-4 relegated (Serie A has 20 teams → 4 relegated). Serie B: 20 teams, top-4 promoted, bottom-4 relegated. Keep it simple: champion = first; relegated = last 4 (only for Serie A/B; note in output).\n- Cups: no standings; provide champion detection instead. Copa do Brasil: final = max round for that season; winner = aggregate score over final legs (2 legs home/away, aggregate; if tie... penalties not in data — just report aggregate and if equal, note \"decided on penalties (not in data)\"). Hmm, aggregate ties: e.g. 2018 final Cruzeiro 1-0, 0-0 → Cruzeiro. Let me implement: aggregate goals across final matches; if equal, report as tied on aggregate (unknown winner from data)... Actually wait — 2017 final? Let me not over-engineer: compute aggregate; if tie, pick... hmm. For Libertadores: 'final' stage exists in data (stages: 'final', 'group stage', 'quarterfinals', 'round of 16', 'semifinals'). 2018 final: Boca 2-2 River (first leg) then River 3-1 Boca (second leg, at Madrid) → River wins 5-3 aggregate. Data has both legs under stage 'final'? Let me check.\n\n- Libertadores group stage mini-tables: skip (too complex, low value). If asked \"standings Libertadores\", respond that it's a knockout cup and give the bracket/finals instead. Bracket tool: \"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\" → list knockout-stage matches by stage. I'll implement `bracket(competition, season)` returning knockout rounds for cups.\n\n**Player data (FIFA)**: \n- `find_player(name=...)` — substring, case-insensitive, accent-folded. \"Gabriel Barbosa\" not in data (FIFA 19 — he's at Inter Milan as \"Gabigol\"? Let me check \"Gabigol\"). Whatever — search handles substrings.\n- `top_players(nationality='Brazil', club=..., position=..., limit=N)` sorted by Overall.\n- `players_at_club(club)` — resolve club name to FIFA club strings (FIFA club names: \"Flamengo\", \"Grêmio\", \"Atlético Mineiro\"...). Need a mapping from our canonical team to FIFA club string(s). Approach: for each FIFA club, normalize its name with the same base() function and try to resolve against the club registry. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → base \"atleticomineiro\" → alias → club Atlético-MG. \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\" → club Grêmio-RS. \"Sport Club do Recife\" → alias → sport|PE. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → \"americafc\" → alias → america|MG. Nice — the alias map makes this work. But there are ~700 FIFA clubs worldwide; only Brazilian ones resolve to our match-data clubs (foreign clubs get their own club entries or just stay as FIFA club strings). For player queries, I'll search FIFA club strings directly by name matching (substring after normalization) — \"Flamengo\" → FIFA club \"Flamengo\". And also cross-ref: `get_team_profile` shows players whose FIFA club resolves to the team.\n\nLet me check which Brazilian clubs are in FIFA data and their exact names. Also check Libertadores 'final' stage data and Cup finals. Also Serie B coverage in BR-Football (seasons, counts). Let me also check BR-Football date empties and whether goals are floats/NaN.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d2b4e92001YV7jpFMdgR7RJ6","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_05d2a6d43001k6Mq3jCQfg9rTE"}}
