os Laguna for those keywords — wait no, that check searched for ('Flamengo','Palmeiras','Corinthians','São Paulo','Sao Paulo','Santos','Grêmio','Gremio') and found ['Grêmio', 'Santos', 'Santos Laguna']. So NO Flamengo, NO Palmeiras, NO Corinthians, NO São Paulo in the Club column at all!\n\nWeird but that's the data. FIFA 19 did include Brazilian clubs — Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco... Hmm, actually NO — I recall FIFA 19 had Brasileirão teams but with GENERIC players (fake names) because of licensing issues, EXCEPT... hmm. Actually in FIFA 19, the Brazilian league clubs had real kits but fake players? No wait — FIFA 19 had Brazilian clubs with real squads I think (FIFA had licensing for Brasileirão since FIFA 16 or so but players were generic). Yes! That's right: EA had the Brasileirão license but not the players' union license, so Brazilian club rosters had FAKE players. But this dataset — players at Grêmio, Internacional, Santos etc. exist with 20 Brazilian players each... so SOME clubs have real rosters.\n\nHmm wait, maybe the dataset is FIFA 19 and the fake-player rosters were EXCLUDED (they'd be fake names of Brazilians — actually fake players ARE Brazilian nationality with fake names). The dataset has 18,207 players — full FIFA 19 database minus... FIFA 19 has ~18,000+ players total. Hmm.\n\nWhatever the reason — the data is what it is: no Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo players in the Club field. So \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" would return nothing from this FIFA dataset... unless we handle it differently. Hmm. Let me double check by searching all clubs for these strings directly.\n\nActually wait — maybe the club names are spelled differently, like \"FLA\"? Let me search. Also \"Gabigol\" — Gabriel Barbosa was at Inter Milan in FIFA 19. Let me search names too.\n\nLet me verify what clubs exist containing 'Fla', 'Palmei', 'Corinth', 'São P'.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d2ebbdc0013Kj9KjidM