ian players\", \"highest-rated at Flamengo\") — I implement the capability; data coverage is what it is. The spec's own example output mentions \"Flamengo: 8 players\" — but our actual data doesn't have it. Fine — tools must return properly formatted responses regardless.\n\nThe \"top Brazilian players\" example works: Neymar Jr 92 etc.\n\nNow, the merge of FIFA clubs into clubs registry: FIFA \"Vitória\" (BA) vs Portuguese \"Vitória Guimarães\"/\"Vitória de Setúbal\" — distinct bases (\"vitória\", \"vitóriaguimarães\", \"vitóriadesetúbal\") — after accent folding: vitoria, vitoriaguimaraes, vitoriadesetubal. Bare \"vitoria\" → merge into (vitoria, BA) since Cup has \"Vitória - BA\". Good. But WAIT — \"Vitória\" bare merging to BA: the FIFA Vitória IS the BA one ✓. \n\n\"Santos\" (FIFA, Brazilian) vs \"Santos Laguna\" (Mexican) — bases \"santos\" and \"santosl\" wait \"Santos Laguna\" → tokens [\"santos\",\"laguna\"] → base \"santosl\"? NO — I decided base = concatenation of ALL tokens: \"santoslaguna\". Hmm wait, with no-spaces concatenation: \"Santos Laguna\" → \"santoslaguna\"; \"Sport Club do Recife\" → \"sportclubdorecife\". OK.\n\nSo FIFA \"Santos\" → base \"santos\" state None → merge into (santos, SP) ✓ (bm \"Santos-SP\"; note Cup also has \"Santos - AP\" — different club (Santos-AP). Since (santos, SP) exists with way more records, bare \"santos\" merges there ✓.)\n\nNow — the plan for FIFA club → club registry resolution is just the same normalize+alias+merge pipeline. \n\nOne more check — \"Boavista FC\" (FIFA, Portuguese) vs Cup \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...) - RJ\" (Brazilian): bases \"boavistafc\" vs \"boavistasportclubantigoesporteclubebarreira\" — distinct ✓.\n\n\"CD Nacional\" (FIFA) → \"cdnacional\" vs Libertadores \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacionaluru\", \"Nacional (PAR)\" → \"nacionalpar\" ✓ distinct.\n\n\"Internacional\" (FIFA) → bare \"internacional\" → merge into (internacional, RS) — but Cup has \"Internacional - SC\" (state SC, base internacional). Both (internacional, RS) [from bm] and (internacional, SC) [from Cup] exist → bare merges into the one with more records = RS ✓ correct (FIFA's Internacional = Inter de Porto Alegre).\n\nEdge: bare merge picks club with most MATCH records. FIFA clubs have no matches, so merging works off match-record counts. ✓.\n\nNow let me also think about **\"Athletico\" bare in Libertadores** — alias to (atletico, PR). And **\"Atlético\" bare?** Does it appear? In Libertadores team list I saw 'Atlético-MG', 'Atlético-PR' with state. Let me grep Libertadores for bare 'Atlético'. Also Cup 'Atlético - AC' etc all have states. I'll handle bare \"atletico\" via most-records merge → MG. Fine either way.\n\n**Now, the date question for standings**: For authoritative-source standings, no cross-source merge needed. ✓.\n\n**Half-time / stats**: BR-Football has ht_result/at_result, corners, shots, attacks. Expose a `match_statistics` tool? Or include in match records when present. When the canonical record comes from bm (preferred over BR-Football), extended stats are lost. Hmm. Options: (a) join BR-Football stats onto canonical records by (date, home, away) — the dates DO align within bm↔BR-Football? BR-Football 2015 Serie A: 378 matches vs bm 380 — near-complete and likely same dates (both derived from official calendars?). Dates: bm \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" vs BR-F \"2023-09-24\" — date-only compare. Risk: some differ. (b) Keep it simple: `search_matches(source=...)` includes BR-Football records with their stats when the user asks; plus a dedicated tool `match_statistics(team, season)` querying BR-Football directly.\n\nI'll do (b) — simpler, honest. Actually, let me reconsider... The canonical index already picks ONE source per (competition, season). Extended stats from BR-Football: I'll attach them to canonical matches where joinable (same date + same bases), storing in the Match record as optional fields. Join rate will be high (BR-Football 2015-2019 Serie A ~complete). For seasons where BR-Football IS the canonical source (2022-2023), stats are already there. Actually — hmm, is the join worth it? The queries: \"average corners\", \"halftime results\"... not in the required capabilities. The required capabilities mention match stats only in passing (BR-Football columns). I'll include a `stats` sub-tool over BR-Football directly, and ALSO attach stats to canonical records via the date+teams join. It's cheap to implement: build dict from BR-Football rows keyed by (date, home_base, away_base) → stats; when emitting canonical records, look up. ~30 lines. OK let me do the join but keep it best-effort.\n\nHmm wait, actually — for BR-Football Serie A 2021 with 491 matches (duplicates!), the join dict might have dupes; first-wins. Whatever, best-effort.\n\n**Libertadores stage translation**: \"group stage\" → \"Group Stage\", \"round of 16\", \"quarterfinals\", \"semifinals\", \"final\". Fine as-is.\n\n**Cup round naming**: rounds 1-8. What do they mean? 2012: 6 rounds, R6 = final (2 matches). 2013+: 8 rounds... For 2013, 158 matches across 8 rounds. Actual 2013 CdB: 87 matches total? 158 > 87. Hmm — 2013 Copa do Brasil had 87 matches? The 2013 CdB: 84-87 matches. But dataset has 158 for 2013?! Maybe includes preliminary rounds or the \"Copa do Brasil\" for them includes state-cup qualifiers?? Or two-legged early rounds (2013+ CdB had two-legged first rounds!). Yes — from 2013 onward, early CdB rounds were two-legged. So 158 plausible. The FINAL for 2013: R8 = 2 matches? Let me trust \"max round with ≤2 matches = final\" heuristic. For 2021: max round 4 with 16 matches → not a final → incomplete season.\n\nHmm wait, actually for 2013 let me verify R8 count — earlier I only printed 2019 R8 (2 matches: Athletico-PR x Internacional — that WAS the 2019 final ✓). Let me check all seasons' max-round match counts to validate the heuristic. Quick check now.\n\n**Now the standings for 2021 CdB**: Cup file 2021 incomplete (108 matches, ends R4 with NA scores). BR-Football CdB 2021: 111 matches. Similar. Since cup standings don't exist anyway, irrelevant. For \"Copa do Brasil 2022-2023\", BR-Football is the only source ✓.\n\n**Champion detection for Copa do Brasil**: max round ≤2 matches → aggregate winner across those matches. E.g. 2019: Athletico-PR 1-0 home, away 1-2 → aggregate 2-2 → penalties (Athletico won IRL). Report \"tied on aggregate (2-2); winner not determinable from scores alone (penalties)\". Hmm — could hardcode actual winners? No — compute from data honestly. But wait — for the 2019 example the user would get \"unknown\". Acceptable for demo; honest.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe for cup finals I can also apply away-goals rule? CdB doesn't use away goals; Libertadores finals don't either (before 2022... 2022 Libertadores still no away goals in finals? Actually CONMEBOL scrapped away goals in 2021+? For 2021-2022 Libertadores knockout ties, away goals were abolished (2021+). For 2013-2019 they existed in knockout rounds but NOT in finals. So: finals → aggregate, tie → penalties → indeterminate from data. Knockout rounds → aggregate with away goals for pre-2021 — too deep. Keep: finals only.\n\n**Libertadores champion**: stage == 'final' matches per season; aggregate. 2019: single match (Flamengo 2-1 River) ✓. 2020: single (Palmeiras 1-0 Santos) ✓. 2013: 2-2 tie → indeterminate (Atlético-MG won on penalties IRL). Report honestly.\n\n**Now — \"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\"** → team_profile tool: competitions list from canonical index + player counts from FIFA. ✓\n\n**Derbies**: hardcode famous pairs (normalized base pairs with state where needed):\n- Fla-Flu: Flamengo-RJ x Fluminense-RJ\n- Clássico dos Milhões: Flamengo-RJ x Vasco-RJ\n- Gre-Nal: Grêmio-RS x Internacional-RS\n- Choque-Rei: Palmeiras-SP x São Paulo-SP\n- Derby Paulista: Palmeiras-SP x Corinthians-SP; also Corinthians x São Paulo (Majestoso)\n- Clássico Alvinegro? (Bahia x Vitória = Ba-Vi)\n- Clássico Mineiro: Atlético-MG x Cruzeiro-MG\n- Clássico dos Gigantes? (Coritiba x Athletico-PR = Atletiba)\n- Atlético-GO x Goiás (Clássico do Cerrado)? \nKeep a dict: name → (clubA, clubB). Tool `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` lists matches between any pair. \n\n**\"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\"** → standings already gives GF leader; team_stats tool per team; also could add `top_scoring_teams(competition, season)`. Standings covers it — I'll make standings include GF/GA/GD and sort. The LLM can answer from standings. But direct tool is nice: skip, standings suffices. Actually the success criteria: \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" — standings(2023) gives it. ✓\n\n**\"Compare the 2018 and 2019 seasons\"** — LLM calls standings twice + avg_goals twice. ✓\n\n**\"Compare Palmeiras and Santos head-to-head\"** → head_to_head tool ✓.\n\n**\"Which team has the best away record?\"** → team_records tool with venue=away across competition/all → I'll implement `best_records(competition, season, venue)` hmm — simpler: `team_stats` per team, plus a `league_table`-like tool `team_records(competition, season, venue)` that returns sorted by points/win-rate. I'll fold into standings with a venue param? Standings(home/away split) — nice: standings(competition, season, venue='away') → away table. ✓\n\n**Tool list final** (FastMCP tools):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=50)` — canonical index.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — matches + summary + per-competition breakdown.\n3. `team_record(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, GF/GA, win rates, matches count; supports multiple seasons (season=None → all).\n4. `standings(competition='Brasileirão Serie A', season, venue=None)` — league table; errors nicely for cups (suggest champion/bracket).\n5. `champion(competition, season)` — league: standings[0]; cup: finals aggregate.\n6. `find_player(name=None, club=None, nationality=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=50)` — FIFA search.\n7. `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)` — sorted by overall.\n8. `club_players(club)` — alias-ish of find_player(club=...) with summary (count, avg rating, positions). Maybe merge into find_player with a summary. Keep separate? find_player covers it. I'll have `players_at_club(club)` returning summary + roster. Fine.\n9. `team_profile(team)` — cross-file: aliases, competitions played, seasons, W/D/L all-time (canonical), goals, notable FIFA players at club.\n10. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` — by margin, then goals.\n11. `season_averages(competition, season=None)` — goals/match, home win %, draw %, avg home/away goals; across all seasons if season=None.\n12. `last_match(team, opponent=None)` — most recent.\n13. `derby_matches(season=None, competition=None, limit=None)` — famous derby pairs.\n14. `competition_info(competition=None)` — seasons, match counts, sources; maybe champions list per season.\n15. `list_teams(competition=None, season=None)` — teams in competition/season.\n16. `match_statistics(team=None, season=None, opponent=None, limit=50)` — BR-Football extended stats (corners, shots, attacks, halftime).\n17. `bracket(competition, season)` — cup knockout rounds listing.\n18. `relegated_teams(competition, season)` — bottom 4 of Serie A/B standings. (Or fold into standings output with relegation zone marked. Fold in — standings marks champion & relegated. Keep separate tool? The question \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → standings output includes relegation markers. I'll fold into standings, no separate tool.)\n\nAlso maybe `resolve_team(name)` helper returning canonical name + variants — useful for LLM disambiguation. Yes, add.\n\nHmm, that's 17 tools — plenty. All JSON-serializable outputs (lists of dicts / dicts).\n\n**Knowledge graph**: the overview mentions \"knowledge graph interface\". I'll frame TeamRegistry as the graph: nodes (Club entities with ids), edges (match edges, player→club edges). Add tool `team_graph(team)`? team_profile essentially returns the node + its edges. I'll structure `graph.py`... Actually — I'll keep it honest: a `knowledge_graph(team)` tool that returns nodes/edges around a team (matches as edges to opponents, players linked). Hmm, that's basically team_profile. I'll make team_profile return a graph-shaped structure {node: {...}, edges: [...]}. Enough.\n\nWait — the prompts.txt mentions \"Use Neo4j\" in a sample prompt variant, but the actual TASK.md (the real spec) doesn't require Neo4j. The task says \"Read TASK.md and implement everything it asks for\" — TASK.md = the spec = MCP server with knowledge-graph interface (loosely). No database requirement. I'll implement in-memory (data is 30k rows — perfect for memory). I'll note the design choice in README.\n\n**Performance**: load all CSVs at startup (cached singleton). 18k FIFA rows + 24k match rows — csv module loads in <1s. Queries: linear scans — fine. Simple lookups < 2s ✓ (well under). \n\n**Project layout** (all in current dir):\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n    __init__.py          # package doc, version\n    models.py            # dataclasses: ClubRef/TeamVariant?, Match, Player, StandingRow, ExtStats\n    normalize.py         # accent fold, team normalization, aliases, date parsing, competition normalization\n    registry.py          # ClubRegistry (clubs, variants, display names), build from data\n    loaders.py           # load each CSV → raw records; Dataset class assembling canonical index + players\n    service.py           # query functions (pure, no MCP) — search_matches, head_to_head, standings, ...\n    server.py            # FastMCP wiring + entry point (python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp.server or python server.py)\nserver.py                # thin entry script at repo root\npyproject.toml\ntests/\n    conftest.py          # fixtures: dataset (module scope), service\n    test_normalize.py    # BDD GWT\n    test_matches.py\n    test_teams.py\n    test_players.py\n    test_competitions.py\n    test_stats.py\n    test_server.py       # MCP tool registration/smoke (call tool functions directly)\n    features/            # Gherkin .feature files documenting scenarios? The spec says \"Use BDD test scenarios\" with Gherkin examples. I'll add a features/ dir with .feature files for documentation, and implement GWT-style pytest. (No pytest-bdd dependency needed — but I could pip install pytest-bdd... The task says make tests pass; pytest-bdd would make Gherkin executable. Hmm — \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\" is from prompts.txt (not binding). TASK.md says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with a Gherkin sketch. I'll write GWT-structured pytest classes with Given/When/Then sections as comments + gherkin .feature files. That's defensible BDD-style without extra deps. Actually — let me just install pytest-bdd and make the .feature files EXECUTABLE. That's the most faithful reading. Hmm, but pytest-bdd adds fragility. Middle ground: GWT-structured pytest (Given/When/Then docstrings and names). The spec's own sketch is just Gherkin text in the doc, not a mandate for a framework. I'll do structured pytest with clear given/when/then phases + include .feature reference files. Decision: pytest with BDD naming; no extra deps.\n\nLet me now also double-check the Cup max-round-final heuristic across seasons, and check Libertadores bare 'Athletico'/'Atlético' rows, then write code.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d304086001rFaIG4X47BqYqU","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_05d2ebe1e00199FSgLZQa8PNqa"}}
