{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788270004257,"sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_69168bcb49804b70924b3b4d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\ndef standings(rows, season):\n    st = {}\n    for r in rows:\n        if r['season'] != season: continue\n        h, a, hg, ag = r['home_team'], r['away_team'], int(r['home_goal']), int(r['away_goal'])\n        for t in (h, a): st.setdefault(t, [0]*8)  # P W D L GF GA GD Pts\n        st[h][0]+=1; st[a][0]+=1\n        st[h][5]+=ag; st[a][5]+=hg; st[h][4]+=hg; st[a][4]+=ag\n        if hg>ag: st[h][1]+=1; st[a][3]+=1; st[h][7]+=3\n        elif hg<ag: st[a][1]+=1; st[h][3]+=1; st[a][7]+=3\n        else: st[h][2]+=1; st[a][2]+=1; st[h][7]+=1; st[a][7]+=1\n    for t,v in st.items(): v[6]=v[4]-v[5]\n    return sorted(st.items(), key=lambda kv:(-kv[1][7],-kv[1][6],-kv[1][4]))\n\nwith open('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nfor season in ('2019','2020','2021'):\n    tab = standings(rows, season)\n    print(f\"== {season} ==\")\n    for i,(t,v) in enumerate(tab[:3],1): print(f\"  {i}. {t} {v[7]}pts P{v[0]} W{v[1]} D{v[2]} L{v[3]} GF{v[4]} GA{v[5]}\")\n    print(\"  relegated:\", [t for t,v in tab[-4:]])\nEOF"},"output":"== 2019 ==\n  1. Flamengo-RJ 90pts P38 W28 D6 L4 GF86 GA37\n  2. Palmeiras-SP 74pts P38 W21 D11 L6 GF61 GA32\n  3. Santos-SP 74pts P38 W22 D8 L8 GF60 GA33\n  relegated: ['Cruzeiro-MG', 'Chapecoense-SC', 'Csa-AL', 'Avai-SC']\n== 2020 ==\n  1. Flamengo-RJ 71pts P38 W21 D8 L9 GF68 GA48\n  2. Internacional-RS 70pts P38 W20 D10 L8 GF61 GA35\n  3. Atletico-MG 68pts P38 W20 D8 L10 GF64 GA45\n  relegated: ['Vasco da Gama-RJ', 'Goias-GO', 'Coritiba-PR', 'Botafogo-RJ']\n== 2021 ==\n  1. Atletico-MG 84pts P38 W26 D6 L6 GF67 GA34\n  2. Flamengo-RJ 71pts P38 W21 D8 L9 GF69 GA36\n  3. Palmeiras-SP 66pts P38 W20 D6 L12 GF58 GA43\n  relegated: ['Gremio-RS', 'Bahia-BA', 'Sport-PE', 'Chapecoense-SC']\n","metadata":{"output":"== 2019 ==\n  1. Flamengo-RJ 90pts P38 W28 D6 L4 GF86 GA37\n  2. Palmeiras-SP 74pts P38 W21 D11 L6 GF61 GA32\n  3. Santos-SP 74pts P38 W22 D8 L8 GF60 GA33\n  relegated: ['Cruzeiro-MG', 'Chapecoense-SC', 'Csa-AL', 'Avai-SC']\n== 2020 ==\n  1. Flamengo-RJ 71pts P38 W21 D8 L9 GF68 GA48\n  2. Internacional-RS 70pts P38 W20 D10 L8 GF61 GA35\n  3. Atletico-MG 68pts P38 W20 D8 L10 GF64 GA45\n  relegated: ['Vasco da Gama-RJ', 'Goias-GO', 'Coritiba-PR', 'Botafogo-RJ']\n== 2021 ==\n  1. Atletico-MG 84pts P38 W26 D6 L6 GF67 GA34\n  2. Flamengo-RJ 71pts P38 W21 D8 L9 GF69 GA36\n  3. Palmeiras-SP 66pts P38 W20 D6 L12 GF58 GA43\n  relegated: ['Gremio-RS', 'Bahia-BA', 'Sport-PE', 'Chapecoense-SC']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\ndef standings(rows, season):\n    st = {}\n    for r in rows:\n        if r['season'] != season: continue\n        h, a, hg, ag = r['home_team'], r['away_team'], int(r['home_goal']), int(r['away_goal'])\n        for t in (h, a): st.setdefault(t, [0]*8)  # P W D L GF GA GD Pts\n        st[h][0]+=1; st[a][0]+=1\n        st[h][5]+=ag; st[a][5]+=hg; st[h][4]+=hg; st[a][4]+=ag\n        if hg>ag: st[h][1]+=1; st[a][3]+=1; st[h][7]+=3\n        elif hg<ag: st[a][1]+=1; st[h][3]+=1; st[a][7]+=3\n        else: st[h][2]+=1; st[a][2]+=1; st[h][7]+=1; st[a][7]+=1\n    for t,v in st.items(): v[6]=v[4]-v[5]\n    return sorted(st.items(), key=lambda kv:(-kv[1][7],-kv[1][6],-kv[1][4]))\n\nwith open('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nfor season in ('2019','2020','2021'):\n    tab = standings(rows, season)\n    print(f\"== {season} ==\")\n    for i,(t,v) in enumerate(tab[:3],1): print(f\"  {i}. {t} {v[7]}pts P{v[0]} W{v[1]} D{v[2]} L{v[3]} GF{v[4]} GA{v[5]}\")\n    print(\"  relegated:\", [t for t,v in tab[-4:]])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788270004249,"end":1788270004255}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"All checks confirm my design:\n\n1. Cup finals: max round with count 2 = final, for 2012-2020. 2021 incomplete (max round 4, count 16) → no final. ✓ Heuristic: a season's final = highest round if that round has ≤ 2 matches; else \"incomplete\".\n\n2. Bare \"Athletico\" in Libertadores = Athletico-PR (2019, 2020, 2022 + NA final row). My alias: bare \"athletico\" → (atletico, PR). ✓ (Athletico-PR played Libertadores 2019, 2020, 2022 — correct!)\n\n3. NA-score rows: 2 in Libertadores, 16 in Cup (2021). Keep with goals=None, exclude from stats.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the implementation. Let me define the architecture concretely:\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n    __init__.py\n    normalize.py     # fold_accents, parse_date, parse_int, normalize_team -> TeamName(base, state), competition mapping, aliases\n    models.py        # Match, Player dataclasses (frozen? plain), StandingRow\n    registry.py      # ClubRegistry: variants -> clubs, resolve(query) -> Club list\n    dataset.py       # load everything, build canonical index (authoritative source per comp/season), attach ext stats; Dataset singleton\n    service.py       # query API (pure functions over Dataset)\n    server.py        # FastMCP tools wrapping service; main()\nserver.py            # root entry: from brazilian_soccer_mcp.server import main; main()\npyproject.toml\nREADME.md           # update\ntests/\n    conftest.py\n    test_normalize.py\n    test_dataset.py     # loading + dedup + coverage (all 6 files)\n    test_match_queries.py\n    test_team_queries.py\n    test_player_queries.py\n    test_competition_queries.py\n    test_statistics.py\n    test_server.py\n```\n\n### normalize.py details\n\n```python\nUF_CODES = {27 states}\n\ndef fold_accents(s): NFKD → ascii\n\ndef parse_date(s) -> date | None  # formats: %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S, %Y-%m-%d, %d/%m/%Y, %d/%m/%Y %H:%M\ndef parse_int(v) -> int | None    # handles '2', 2, '2.0', 'NA', '-', ''\n\nTEAM_ALIASES: dict[str, tuple[str, str|None]]  # base -> (base, state)\n  vascodagama -> (vasco, RJ)\n  atleticomineiro -> (atletico, MG)\n  atleticoparanaense -> (atletico, PR)\n  athleticoparanaense -> (atletico, PR)\n  atleticogoianiense -> (atletico, GO)\n  athleticopr?? (never bare-with-state since state already PR → \"athletico|PR\" → base alias athletico->atletico keeps state) \n  athletico (bare) -> (atletico, PR)   # handled before general rule since general rule would just rename base\n  atletico (bare) -> leave as (atletico, None) → merge-by-records → MG\n  sportclubdorecife -> (sport, PE)\n  sportrecife -> (sport, PE)\n  cearascar -> (ceara, CE)\n  americafc -> (america, MG)\n  americafcminasgerais -> (america, MG)\n  americafcnatal -> (america, RN)\n  americadenatal -> (america, RN)\n  ecbahia -> (bahia, BA)\n  inter? no — 'inter' base isn't used; bm uses 'Internacional-RS' full.\n  redbullbragantino stays (base redbullbragantino) — BR-Football \"Red Bull Bragantino\" same ✓\n  cuiaba stays\n  athleticocasad...? no.\n  gremio? stays.\n  What about \"Atletico Goianiense\" vs \"Atletico-GO\": alias atleticogoianiense -> (atletico, GO) ✓\n  \"Atlético Cearense - CE\" → base atleticocearense state CE — distinct club, no alias ✓\n  \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\" → base athleticoparanaense + state PR → alias (atletico, PR) ✓\n  \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" → atleticomineiro + MG → (atletico, MG) ✓\n  \"São Paulo\" bare → (saopaulo, None) → merge to SP club ✓\n  Paraná (FIFA \"Paraná\", bm \"Parana-PR\") → (parana, None) → merge (parana, PR) ✓\n  \"Botafogo RJ\" → (botafogo, RJ) ✓\n  \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → (vascodagama→vasco, RJ) ✓\n```\n\nGeneral algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef normalize_team(raw: str) -> tuple[base, state]:\n    s = fold_accents(raw).lower()\n    s = s.replace('(', ' ').replace(')', ' ')   # keep content as tokens\n    s = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", ' ', s)           # punctuation → space\n    toks = s.split()\n    state = None\n    if toks and toks[-1].upper() in UF_CODES:\n        state = toks[-1].upper(); toks = toks[:-1]\n    base = ''.join(toks)\n    # alias: if base in ALIASES: newbase, newstate = ALIASES[base]; base=newbase; state = state or newstate\n    #   special: bare 'athletico'/'atletico' with no state:\n    #   ALIASES['athletico'] = ('atletico', 'PR') — but 'Athletico-PR' also hits this alias; state PR given → same result ✓\n    #   ALIASES['atletico'] not defined → bare atletico stays (atletico, None) → record-count merge → MG ✓\n    return base, state\n```\n\nWait, careful with alias semantics: `alias[base] = (target_base, target_state)`; apply as: base → target_base; state = state if state else target_state. For \"athletico\"→(\"atletico\",\"PR\"): \"Athletico-PR\" (state=PR) → (atletico, PR) ✓; bare \"Athletico\" → (atletico, PR) ✓. For \"atleticomineiro\"→(\"atletico\",\"MG\"): \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" state MG → (atletico, MG) ✓; FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" no state → (atletico, MG) ✓. \n\nBut hmm — \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" — tokens [\"atletico\",\"mineiro\",\"mg\"] → state MG, base \"atleticomineiro\" → alias (atletico, MG) ✓.\n\nAnother: \"Internacional - SC\" → (internacional, SC) — distinct club from (internacional, RS) ✓.\n\n\"Sport - PE\" → (sport, PE) ✓. FIFA \"Sport Club do Recife\" → base \"sportclubdorecife\" state None → alias (sport, PE) ✓.\n\n\"EC Bahia\" → \"ecbahia\" → (bahia, BA) ✓.\n\nFIFA \"Botafogo\" → (botafogo, None) → merge → RJ ✓ (botafogo|RJ vs botafogo|PB — RJ has more records ✓).\n\nFIFA \"Santos\" → (santos, None) → merge → SP ✓ (santos|SP >> santos|AP).\n\nFIFA \"Vitória\" → (vitoria, None) → merge → BA ✓.\n\nFIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → base \"cearascar...\" wait tokens: [\"ceara\",\"sporting\",\"club\"] → base \"cearasportingclub\"! Not \"cearasc\". I need alias \"cearasportingclub\" → (ceara, CE). Right.\n\nFIFA \"Chapecoense\" → (chapecoense, None) → merge → (chapecoense, SC) ✓.\nFIFA \"Grêmio\" → (gremio, None) → merge → RS ✓ (Cup \"Gremio Esportivo Sapucaiense - RS\" is base \"gremioesportivosapucaiense\" — distinct ✓; \"Grêmio Barueri - SP\" base \"gremiobarueri\" distinct ✓).\nFIFA \"Cruzeiro\" → merge → MG ✓. \"Fluminense\" → RJ ✓. \"Internacional\" → RS ✓. \"Bahia\" → BA ✓. \"Paraná\" → PR ✓. \"Atlético Paranaense\" → alias (atletico, PR) ✓. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → tokens [americafc, minas, gerais] → base \"americafcminasgerais\" → alias (america, MG) ✓.\n\nForeign FIFA clubs: \"Atlético Madrid\" → (atleticomadrid, None) → no same-base state club → own club ✓ distinct from atletico|*. ✓ Good.\n\n\"Alanyaspor\" etc. — own clubs, harmless.\n\nNow **the merge step** (base-only → state-qualified club with same base, most match records; if no state-qualified club exists, stays own club). \n\nAnd **query resolution** `resolve(query) -> list[Club]`:\n- normalize query → (base, state)\n- if state: clubs with exact (base, state) → if none, fall back to base-only club (base, None) → e.g. query \"Athletico Paranaense\" → alias → (atletico, PR) direct hit ✓. Query \"Boavista\" → (boavista, None) — FIFA Boavista FC → base \"boavistafc\" hmm! Query \"Boavista\" (base \"boavista\") vs club bases \"boavistafc\" (FIFA) and \"boavistasportclubantigo...\" (Cup). Neither matches exactly! Containment fallback needed: clubs whose base contains query base or vice versa. \"boavistafc\".contains(\"boavista\") ✓. So resolution tiers:\n  1. exact (base, state)\n  2. exact base (any state) — if multiple, order by record count (all returned, best first)\n  3. containment: club.base contains query.base or query.base contains club.base (len ≥ 4 to avoid noise) — ordered by record count\n  4. also check FIFA club-string containment (for \"Alanyaspor\"-type) — actually FIFA clubs go through the same registry (each FIFA club becomes a Club entity), so covered.\n- Empty → return [] with helpful error at tool level.\n\nFor **stats/matches**, use ALL resolved clubs? If query is \"Flamengo\" (no state): tier-2 finds (flamengo, RJ) and (flamengo, PI) — using both would mix! Better: tools use the BEST match (most records) but warn about ambiguity in the response. I'll have resolve() return ranked list; tools take primary + note alternates in output (\"Ambiguity note: also matched Flamengo-PI\"). For head_to_head both teams resolved similarly.\n\nHmm — but for teams like \"Palmeiras\" (unique club) no ambiguity. For \"America\": (america, MG) best, also (america, RN) — note. Good behavior.\n\n**Club identity for matches**: each Match stores home_club_id/away_club_id (from variant→club resolution at load). Canonical matches from authoritative sources.\n\n**Records counting for merge priority**: count matches per club (as home or away) across ALL sources (before canonical selection — more data for ranking; or after? After canonical selection is \"the data we serve\" — use canonical counts. Hmm, at merge time I need counts BEFORE building canonical... circular. Use all-source raw match counts for merge ranking. Fine.)\n\nWait, actually there's a subtle ordering problem: FIFA clubs are loaded and registered as clubs; their \"match records\" = 0, so a bare FIFA variant never out-ranks. And merge happens only for state=None variants. Let me sequence the build:\n\n1. Parse all match rows from 5 files → raw rows with (date, home_raw, away_raw, hg, ag, comp, season, round, stage, source, venue/stadium).\n2. Normalize team names → (base, state) variants. \n3. Register ALL variants + FIFA club variants into registry: club key (base, state) → Club(id, key, variants set, match_count=0, player_count=0, display).\n4. Count matches per club key (from raw match rows).\n5. Merge pass: every (base, None) club with 0... no wait — (base, None) club MERGES into same-base state-qualified club with most matches IF any exists; else stays. When merging, the (base,None) club's matches attach to the target; variants union; the (base,None) key remains as an alias in resolve lookup.\n   - CAREFUL: after merging, the source (base,None) club should no longer be a separate entity. Registry keeps map variant_key → club.\n   - ALSO: state-qualified clubs never merge with each other. ✓\n6. Recompute match club attachments via variant → club.\n7. Build canonical index: group raw matches by (competition, season); pick authoritative source per group by priority [bm, cup_file, libertadores_file, novo, brfootball] hmm — priority order: for Serie A: bm > novo > BR-Football. For CdB: cup_file > BR-Football. General: bm=1, cup=1, lib=1, novo=2, brf=3? But Serie B/C: only brf → picked. Serie A 2003-2011: bm none → novo. Serie A 2022-2023: brf. CdB 2022-2023: brf. Libertadores: lib file only. Wait — should BR-Football Serie A 2015 (378) ever be preferred over bm (380)? No — priority order handles: bm always for 2012-2021.\n   Hmm — one concern: bm 2012-2021 complete; but what if bm MISSED a match that novo has? bm = 4180 = 11×380 exactly — complete. ✓\n8. Attach extended stats: BR-Football rows keyed (date, home_base→club?, away_base→club?) — use CLUB ids: (date, home_club, away_club) → ext stats. Apply to canonical matches with same key. Hmm — base-level or club-level? Club-level (post-merge) — better recall (\"Vasco\" vs \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\" unify to club vasco|RJ). ✓\n9. FIFA players: each player's club string → variant → club (if resolvable — foreign clubs become their own club entities; that's fine, they're in the registry). Player count per club.\n\nThen service functions operate over: canonical matches list, raw per-source matches (for source-specific queries), clubs registry, players list.\n\n**Match record fields**: date (ISO str + date obj), home/away display names, home/away club ids, goals (int|None), competition, season (int), round (str|None), stage (str|None), source, stadium (novo only), ext (corners/shots/attacks/halftime | None). Also `has_score`.\n\n**Display names**: curated dict for major clubs keyed by club key:\n```\n(atletico, MG): \"Atlético Mineiro\"  — hmm, or \"Atlético-MG\"? The spec examples use simple names: \"Palmeiras\", \"Santos\". For city-shared names, state matters. I'll display \"Atlético Mineiro (MG)\"? Let me use common Brazilian shorthand WITH state for ambiguous bases and plain otherwise:\n  palmeiras|SP → \"Palmeiras\", flamengo|RJ → \"Flamengo\", corinthians|SP → \"Corinthians\", saopaulo|SP → \"São Paulo\", santos|SP → \"Santos\", gremio|RS → \"Grêmio\", internacional|RS → \"Internacional\", vasco|RJ → \"Vasco da Gama\", botafogo|RJ → \"Botafogo\", fluminense|RJ → \"Fluminense\", atletico|MG → \"Atlético Mineiro\", atletico|PR → \"Athletico Paranaense\", atletico|GO → \"Atlético Goianiense\", cruzeiro|MG → \"Cruzeiro\", sport|PE → \"Sport Recife\", vitoria|BA → \"Vitória\", bahia|BA → \"Bahia\", ceara|CE → \"Ceará\", chapecoense|SC → \"Chapecoense\", parana|PR → \"Paraná\", america|MG → \"América Mineiro\", america|RN → \"América RN\", fortaleza|CE → \"Fortaleza\", goias|GO → \"Goiás\", coritiba|PR → \"Coritiba\", avai|SC → \"Avaí\", figueirense|SC → \"Figueirense\", cuiaba|MT → \"Cuiabá\", redbullbragantino|SP → \"Red Bull Bragantino\", pontepreta|SP → \"Ponte Preta\", portuguesa|SP → \"Portuguesa\", nautico|PE → \"Náutico\", santacruz|PE → \"Santa Cruz\", juventude|RS → \"Juventude\", csa|AL → \"CSA\", criciuma|SC → \"Criciúma\", joinville|SC → \"Joinville\", atletico|AC → \"Atlético Acreano\"... \n```\nFallback: prettify the most-frequent raw variant (title-case with accents preserved? I'll store the most common raw name and use it directly). Keep curated map for ~35 majors, fallback = most common raw variant name.\n\n**Competition display names**: \"Brasileirão Serie A\", \"Brasileirão Serie B\", \"Brasileirão Serie C\", \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Copa Libertadores\". Map inputs: \"serie a\"/\"brasileirao\"/\"série a\" → Serie A. Resolve via normalized comparison: fold+lower+strip non-alnum. Also \"libertadores\", \"copa do brasil\", \"cdb\", \"serie b\", \"serie c\", \"b series\"... I'll write a resolver returning canonical name + accepted aliases list.\n\n**Standings computation**:\n- Filter canonical matches: competition, season, has_score.\n- Group by club (home/away). For each club: matches, W/D/L, GF, GA. Points = 3W+D.\n- Sort: pts desc, wins desc, GD desc, GF desc, name.\n- Annotate: champion (rank 1, leagues only), relegated (last 4) for Serie A/B (20 teams) — mark \"relegation zone\" for bottom 4 if 20 teams; for Serie C skip (different format).\n- Venue param: 'home'/'away' — compute from that club's perspective (home table = home matches only).\n- Cups → raise ValueError with helpful message (champion tool instead).\n\n**Champion (cup)**:\n- Copa do Brasil: final round detection (max round ≤ 2 matches) → aggregate over those; winner = more aggregate goals; tie → indeterminate (penalties note). 2021 → \"data incomplete\".\n- Libertadores: stage == 'final' matches → aggregate; single-match finals straightforward. 2021/2022 → no final data → but 2021 final (Palmeiras x Flamengo, single match) — stage 'final' only 2013-2020 + NA. So 2021 champion → \"final not in dataset\". Honest.\n- League: standings[0].\n\n**Bracket (cup, season)**: rounds/stages in chronological order with matches. For Libertadores: group stage (just count + list? too many) — bracket tool returns knockout stages only (round of 16, QF, SF, final) with match lists; group stage summarized as count. For Copa do Brasil: rounds descending (final first) with matches; rounds named \"Final\" for the max round if ≤2 matches, \"Round N\" otherwise.\n\n**last_match(team, opponent=None)**: max date among canonical matches involving club(s).\n\n**season_averages(competition, season=None)**: over canonical scored matches: matches, total goals, avg goals/match, home wins %, draws %, away wins %, avg home goals, avg away goals.\n\n**biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)**: sort by (margin, total goals) desc; include margin.\n\n**team_record(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)**: canonical scored matches involving club; W/D/L, GF, GA, win %, per-competition breakdown when competition=None.\n\n**head_to_head(a, b, competition=None, season=None)**: matches between (any order), summary from A's perspective + per-competition.\n\n**find_player / top_players / players_at_club**: FIFA. Player fields: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, preferred foot, value, wage, height... Return curated subset: name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey_number, preferred_foot, value, wage + a few key skills (pace-ish? FIFA19 doesn't have Pace column? Header list: ...Crossing, Finishing, HeadingAccuracy, ShortPassing, Volleys, Dribbling, Curve, FKAccuracy, LongPassing, BallControl, Acceleration, SprintSpeed, Agility, Reactions, Balance(?), ... let me include top-level attrs: Finishing, Dribbling, ShortPassing, LongPassing, Reactions, plus Strength etc. Keep: finishing, dribbling, short_passing, long_passing, curve, fk_accuracy, heading_accuracy, volleys, ball_control, acceleration, sprint_speed, agility, reactions... that's a lot. I'll expose a fixed subset in find_player output and a `attributes` dict for detail tool? Keep it lean: name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, foot, value, wage, height, weight, and skills dict with ~10 core ratings when present.\n\nName search: substring on folded names (fold both). \"Gabriel Barbosa\" → no match (not in data) → empty with suggestion. Also try token match: \"Neymar\" matches \"Neymar Jr\" via substring ✓.\n\n**team_profile(team)**: club info (display, states, variants/aliases), competitions+seasons played (canonical), all-time record, biggest wins?, FIFA players count + top 5 by overall at club. Graph-ish structure.\n\n**match_statistics(team=None, opponent=None, season=None, competition=None, limit=50)**: over BR-Football raw rows (all sources? just brf since only it has stats): include corners, shots, attacks, halftime, kickoff time.\n\n**derbies(season=None, competition=None, limit=100)**: DERBY_PAIRS list of (name, clubA_key, clubB_key): \n- \"Fla-Flu\": flamengo|RJ, fluminense|RJ\n- \"Clássico dos Milhões\": flamengo|RJ, vasco|RJ\n- \"Gre-Nal\": gremio|RS, internacional|RS\n- \"Choque-Rei\": palmeiras|SP, saopaulo|SP\n- \"Derby Paulista\": palmeiras|SP, corinthians|SP\n- \"Majestoso\": corinthians|SP, saopaulo|SP\n- \"Clássico Mineiro\": atletico|MG, cruzeiro|MG\n- \"Ba-Vi\": bahia|BA, vitoria|BA\n- \"Atletiba\": atletico|PR, coritiba|PR\n- \"Clássico dos Gigantes da Colina\"? skip\n- \"Clássico Reba\"? nautico|PE, santacruz|PE = \"Clássico dos Clássicos\"? hmm — Náutico x Santa Cruz = \"Clássico dos Clássicos\"; Sport x Náutico = \"Clássico dos Clássicos\"?? no. Sport x Santa Cruz... I'll include Náutico x Sport = \"Clássico dos Emoções\"? I'm not 100% sure of these names. Keep the well-known 9 + \"Clássico Carioca of giants\"... Let me keep: Fla-Flu, Milhões, Gre-Nal, Choque-Rei, Derby Paulista, Majestoso, Mineiro, Ba-Vi, Atletiba. Nine famous ones. ✓\n\n**competition_info()**: for each competition: seasons available, matches per season (canonical), sources used, format (league/cup). With season param: teams count, matches, avg goals, champion.\n\n**list_teams(competition=None, season=None)**: canonical teams with match counts.\n\n**resolve_team(name)**: normalized info + matched clubs with record counts (LLM disambiguation helper).\n\n**search_matches**: filters + pagination (limit default 50, max maybe 500) + total count + note when truncated. Return each match dict: date, time?, competition, season, round/stage, home, away, score \"2-1\" or \"N/A\", source, venue/stadium?, stage. Also winner field.\n\n**MCP server**: FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer\"). Tools = thin wrappers around service functions with docstrings. `main()` runs mcp.run() (stdio). \n\n**pyproject.toml**: project metadata, deps: mcp>=1.0. scripts: brazilian-soccer-mcp = brazilian_soccer_mcp.server:main. Python >=3.10.\n\n**Testing** — BDD GWT structure. conftest fixture `dataset` (module-scoped, loads once) + `service`. Tests mirror spec scenarios:\n\ntest_normalize.py:\n- Given raw names with state suffixes/accents/parens When normalized Then base/state correct\n- aliases: Vasco da Gama → vasco|RJ; Athletico bare → atletico|PR; Atlético Mineiro → atletico|MG; Sport Club do Recife → sport|PE\n- dates: 3 formats parse; NA/'' → None\n- parse_int: '2', '2.0', 'NA' → 2, 2, None\n- competition resolution: 'serie a', 'brasileirão', 'libertadores', 'copa do brasil'\n\ntest_dataset.py:\n- All 6 files loaded: match counts per source; players 18207\n- canonical Serie A 2019 = 380 matches single source (bm)\n- no duplicate (date, home, away) within canonical per competition/season\n- every canonical match has club ids resolved\n- 2022-2023 Serie A present (from BR-Football)\n\ntest_match_queries.py:\n- Flamengo vs Fluminense head-to-head matches have date/score/competition\n- Palmeiras 2023 matches (BR-Football season) > 0\n- date range filter\n- Copa do Brasil finals listing (via stage/round search or bracket)\n- last_match Flamengo Corinthians\n\ntest_team_queries.py:\n- Palmeiras 2023 season stats: W/D/L/GF/GA sane (played ~38)\n- Corinthians home record 2022: matches=19\n- head_to_head Palmeiras x Santos: totals consistent\n- team profile: competitions include multiple\n\ntest_player_queries.py:\n- Brazilian players count 827\n- top Brazilians: Neymar Jr #1 overall 92\n- find_player 'Neymar' → PSG LW\n- players at Grêmio: 20, all nationality Brazil\n- no players at Flamengo (data gap) → graceful empty + note? The tool returns clubs matched... find_player(club='Flamengo') → empty. Test asserts helpful response (empty list + note field).\n\ntest_competition_queries.py:\n- 2019 Brasileirão champion = Flamengo with 90 pts (spec example!) — verify from data: Flamengo 2019: 26W? Let me compute at test time — assert champion club = Flamengo and points == 90? I should verify actual computed value first, then pin test. Let me compute in a scratch run before writing the test. (2019: Flamengo 26W 8D 4L = 86 pts? Official: Flamengo 2019 = 90 pts (28W 6D 4L). Let me verify from bm data.)\n- relegated 2020: the bottom 4 — 2020 official relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Bragantino? 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco da Gama, Bragantino? Hmm — official 2020 Série A relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, RB Bragantino? No — Bragantino finished 10th?? Let me think: 2020 relegated were Coritiba (19th?), Botafogo, Vasco, and... Bragantino was 10th. The four relegated in 2020: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco da Gama, and Sport? No... 2020: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Bragantino — I really am not sure. Compute from data and verify bottom-4 against known: let me check quickly in scratch. 2020 bottom: 17th Vasco (41?), 18th Sport?? Hmm. I'll compute and eyeball against Wikipedia knowledge: 2020 Série A relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco da Gama, Bragantino?? — Bragantino NO (they were 10th, qualified to Sudamericana). The 4 relegated 2020: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Sport Recife? Hmm — Sport was 17th in 2020? No, Sport was relegated in 2021! 2021 relegated: Sport, Bahia? no Bahia was relegated 2021? 2021 relegated: Chapecoense, Sport, Bahia, Grêmio? 2021: Grêmio, Bahia, Sport, Chapecoense relegated ✓ (that I'm confident). 2020: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco da Gama, Bragantino — no wait, I keep flip-flopping. 2020 standings: 17 Coritiba 41? ... The four: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Bragantino? Let me just compute from data in scratch and check plausibility (I'll verify: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco + one of Bragantino/CSA? CSA was 2019. 2019 relegated: CSA, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Avai? 2019: Avaí, CSA, Cruzeiro, Chapecoense ✓ I'm fairly confident. So test 2019 relegated = those four.) I'll verify all with scratch computation.\n- Libertadores 2019 champion = Flamengo (2-1 final) ✓\n- Copa do Brasil 2019 final detection: Athletico-PR vs Internacional, aggregate 2-2 → indeterminate + penalties note\n- standings errors for cups\n\ntest_statistics.py:\n- biggest wins include Santos 8-0? no wait — 2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)? Hmm the spec example mentions it — is it in the data? Libertadores data starts 2013! The spec's example says \"2012-05-27: Santos 8-0 Bolivar (Libertadores)\" — but Libertadores_Matches.csv starts 2013. So that example is illustrative, not data-accurate. My test: biggest wins sorted by margin; assert top margin ≥ 6 and matches sorted.\n- avg goals Serie A 2019 ≈ 2.4-2.7\n- home win rate between 40-55%\n- best away record sane\n\ntest_server.py:\n- server constructs; tool names registered (call list_tools via fastmcp)\n- calling a tool function directly returns JSON-serializable output\n- every tool's return json.dumps-able\n\nAlso a smoke test for entry `python server.py --help`? Running stdio server in test is awkward; instead test `main` exists and `build_server()` returns FastMCP with expected tools. Maybe subprocess smoke: run server with a JSON-RPC initialize message over stdin? That's doable: spawn process, send initialize + tools/list, read response. FastMCP stdio... anyio-based; doable but timing-sensitive. I'll do the lighter version + a `--self-test` CLI flag? Keep: build_server test + direct function calls. Maybe a real stdio round-trip with generous timeout — let me try; if flaky, drop. Actually let me include it but robustly (initialize, tools/list, one tools/call). It validates the full stack. I'll write it carefully with a 30s timeout.\n\nAlso spec performance criteria — add a lightweight perf test: search_matches and standings under 2s / 5s (warm). With in-memory data these are ms. Use time.monotonic asserts — but CI variance... assert generous bounds (2s/5s per spec). OK.\n\n**README** update: describe implementation, architecture, tools, how to run, tests, data notes/licenses.\n\n**Gherkin feature files**: tests/features/*.feature documenting the core scenarios (Given/When/Then) — reference documentation for BDD. I'll write them matching test scenarios.\n\nLet me first verify the standings computation from data (2019 champion pts, 2019 relegated, 2021 relegated) in scratch before pinning test assertions.\n\nAlso decide: `season` param type int or str? Accept both (coerce). date_from/date_to accept 'YYYY-MM-DD'.\n\nOne more design point — **the canonical index and \"source\" for Libertadores NA row**: skipped (unparseable date+goals). And Cup 2021 NA rows: kept with goals=None, marked \"score not recorded\". For head_to_head and standings only scored matches count. For search_matches they appear with score null. OK.\n\nAlso — **Cup 2021 authoritative source**: Cup file (108 matches incl. NA) vs BR-Football (111). Priority picks Cup file. Fine.\n\nAlso — what about **BR-Football Serie A 2021 (491 matches, duplicated)**: never authoritative (bm covers 2021). ✓ For match_statistics tool (raw brf), dupes might show; acceptable, it's raw. Hmm — dedupe raw brf by (date, home_club, away_club) in stats tool? The 491... if dates differ for dupes, can't. Leave.\n\nNow compute verification scratch: 2019 standings from bm; 2021 relegated; 2019 relegated.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d32c7e4001SBH3vytRwVMkYf","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_05d30525e001ctnC4vfesiIo9K"}}
