m brazilian_soccer_mcp.normalize import normalize_team\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nraw, _ = load_matches()\nplayers = load_players()\n# For each source, map base -> set of raw names, for teams with >= 5 matches\nby_source = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(set))\nfor m in raw:\n    by_source[m.source][m.home_team.base].add(m.home_name)\n    by_source[m.source][m.away_team.base].add(m.away_name)\nby_source['fifa'][normalize_team('').base] = set()  # placeholder\nfor p in players:\n    if p.club:\n        by_source['fifa'][normalize_team(p.club).base].add(p.club)\n\n# Cross-source: bases in one source that CONTAIN a base from another source\n# (potential split identities like fortalezafc vs fortaleza)\nbases = {s: set(b for b in bs if b) for s, bs in by_source.items()}\nall_pairs = set()\nfor s1, b1 in bases.items():\n    for s2, b2 in bases.items():\n        if s1 >= s2: continue\n        for x in b1:\n            for y in b2:\n                if x != y and (x in y or y in x) and len(x) >= 4:\n                    all_pairs.add((min(x,y), max(x,y), s1, s2))\nfor x, y, s1, s2 in sorted(all_pairs):\n    # filter: only interesting if both have decent presence\n    print(f\"{x!r} ({s1}) <-> {y!r} ({s2})\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788270622012,"end":1788270622066}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting: \"fortalezafc|\" — a SEPARATE club from \"fortaleza|CE\"! BR-Football spells Fortaleza as \"Fortaleza FC\" (base \"fortalezafc\", no state) while bm spells \"Fortaleza-CE\" (base \"fortaleza\", CE). My alias map has \"fortalezaesporteclube\" but NOT \"fortalezafc\". Also \"bragantino|SP\" — BR-Football spells it \"Bragantino\" (not \"Red Bull Bragantino\")! So (bragantino, SP) is separate from (redbullbragantino, SP) — split identity.\n\nLet me find ALL such split-identity teams: bases in brf 2023 that differ from bm names. Full 2023 Serie A team list vs bm 2022 team list — let me diff them. Teams: gremio|RS ✓ (brf \"Gremio\"), saopaulo|SP ✓, flamengo|RJ ✓, santos|SP ✓, fortalezafc| ✗ SPLIT, cruzeiro|MG ✓, internacional|RS ✓, botafogo|RJ ✓, corinthians|SP ✓, bahia|BA ✓ (brf \"EC Bahia\"→alias ✓), atletico|MG ✓, atletico|PR ✓, goias|GO ✓, america|MG ✓ (brf \"America MG\"), fluminense|RJ ✓, vasco|RJ ✓ (brf \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\"→alias ✓), bragantino|SP ✗ SPLIT (brf \"Bragantino\"), palmeiras|SP ✓, coritiba|PR ✓, cuiaba|MT ✓.\n\nSo two splits in 2023: Fortaleza FC and Bragantino. That explains Palmeiras P37 (their matches vs Fortaleza/Bragantino became cross-club matches vs \"fortalezafc\"/\"bragantino\" — no wait, P37 means Palmeiras only appears in 37 matches — the missing one is a fixture vs fortalezafc or bragantino attributed to those other clubs... no — P counts matches where palmeiras is home or away club. If Palmeiras played \"Fortaleza FC\" (fortalezafc club), that match's away_club = fortalezafc| — so Palmeiras count = 37, missing 1. Hmm, 2 splits × 2 fixtures each = 4 fixtures misattributed; Palmeiras misses 1 (vs Fortaleza FC once? or Bragantino once).\n\nFix: add aliases:\n- \"fortalezafc\" → (\"fortaleza\", \"CE\")\n- \"bragantino\" → (\"redbullbragantino\", \"SP\")\n\nAlso scan ALL brf/other team names for similar split-identity issues systematically. The cleanest check: for every club pair with containment relationship where one is brf-only and other is bm/cup-only... Let me write a quick audit: list clubs with 0 matches in canonical... hmm. Better audit: list all club bases that are substrings of other club bases with same state or no state, focusing on ones appearing in different sources. Let me just dump the top clubs by source and eyeball.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d3c3208001AH0FimpEgkyz9d","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_05d3bcde7001n5rY9Rq8fBBYck"}}
