umn — it showed 'Portuguesa-SP'. But \"portuguesadesportos\" is listed under (Brasileirao_Matches) — contradiction... OH WAIT. The audit pairs are (base_from_source1, base_from_source2) where the pair just needs each base present in that source. Let me recheck: `'portuguesa' (BR-Football-Dataset) <-> 'portuguesadesportos' (Brasileirao_Matches)` — yes it claims bm has portuguesadesportos. Hmm, but the bm home_team list didn't show it... The audit includes AWAY teams too (I add both home and away bases). My earlier dump only listed home_team from bm! So away side might have \"Portuguesa de Desportos-SP\"? Unlikely but possible.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Rather than chasing every pair, let me directly extract the raw names → bases for all brf teams AND all teams in the other sources, and build the alias list for same-club-different-base cases where BOTH bases appear in BRAZILIAN competitions with meaningful overlap. Since perfect entity resolution of every tiny club is impossible, I'll focus on:\n(a) All clubs that appear in bm Serie A 2012-2022 (37 clubs) — make sure brf 2023 Serie A clubs (20) map onto them.\n(b) All clubs in brf Serie B/C that also appear elsewhere under different bases — best effort for the notable ones.\n\nLet me get the precise raw-name → base lists per source for the teams of interest. Let me print for each source: raw name → base for names matching key clubs (Fortaleza, Bragantino, Juventude, Vitória, CSA, Náutico, Portuguesa, Internacional, Paraná, Ceará, Botafogo (PB?), América, Atlético Goianiense, Sport, Bahia, Cuiabá, Avaí, Goiás, Coritiba...).\n\nActually, simpler and more robust: **write an automated same-club detector**. Two bases X, Y from different sources refer to the same club if they share a state (or one has none) AND one base is a \"suffix-extension\" of the other (e.g., fortalezafc vs fortaleza: \"fortalezafc\" starts with \"fortaleza\"; ecvitoria vs vitoria: \"ecvitoria\" ENDS with \"vitoria\"; portuguesadesportos vs port