{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788272793789,"sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_56d89c6f999444be8dc6c6cd","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nimport mcp.server.mcpserver as ms\nsrc = inspect.getsource(ms.MCPServer.call_tool)\nprint(src[:2500])\nEOF"},"output":"    async def call_tool(\n        self, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any], context: Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None = None\n    ) -> CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult:\n        \"\"\"Call a tool by name with arguments.\n\n        Raises:\n            ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n                tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n            UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n                its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n                exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n            MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n        \"\"\"\n        if context is None:\n            context = Context(mcp_server=self, subscriptions=self._subscriptions)\n        return await self._tool_manager.call_tool(name, arguments, context, convert_result=True)\n\n","metadata":{"output":"    async def call_tool(\n        self, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any], context: Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None = None\n    ) -> CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult:\n        \"\"\"Call a tool by name with arguments.\n\n        Raises:\n            ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n                tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n            UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n                its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n                exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n            MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n        \"\"\"\n        if context is None:\n            context = Context(mcp_server=self, subscriptions=self._subscriptions)\n        return await self._tool_manager.call_tool(name, arguments, context, convert_result=True)\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nimport mcp.server.mcpserver as ms\nsrc = inspect.getsource(ms.MCPServer.call_tool)\nprint(src[:2500])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788272793751,"end":1788272793788}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The error paths work (MCP converts exceptions into isError results), but the error MESSAGE is being swallowed: \"Error executing tool standings\" — the actual ValueError message (\"...use champion() or bracket() instead\") isn't surfaced. For \"bad season\", the message shows \"1 validation error for standings\" — that's pydantic rejecting `season: \"abc\"` (int type) at the schema layer, fine.\n\nFor the ValueError case, the tool result content should carry the message so the LLM can self-correct. The content[0].text was \"Error executing tool standings\" — where did the exception text go? In MCP v2, when a tool raises, the server catches and includes str(exc)? Apparently not by default — it says \"Error executing tool {name}\". Let me check the mcp v2 source to see how exceptions are serialized — maybe the message IS included somewhere (e.g., in `structuredContent.error` or additional content items). Let me check quickly.\n\nIf the SDK doesn't propagate messages, best practice for good LLM UX: catch ValueError in the tool wrappers and return {\"error\": msg} as a normal result instead of raising. Let me look at how MCPServer.call_tool serializes exceptions.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d5d56c6001FP7F9JpCtdCz27","sessionID":"ses_fa2db0ef4ffeemTLK4uNaaAd5c","messageID":"msg_0